2026-06-30 15:26:49,385 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-7b5c51fd-09b4-4c57-adc0-a5097186d3b9', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms from the provided publication text and/or PRIDE project description.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: sex\nsdrf_column: characteristics[sex]\nontology: PATO:0000047\ndescription: Biological sex of the donor.\nallowed_values: male, female, intersex, hermaphrodite, anonymized, not available, not applicable\ninstructions: Use one of the fixed values above. Return "not available" if not stated.\n  Return "not applicable" if the sample is from a cell line or organism without defined sex.\n\nterm: age\nsdrf_column: characteristics[age]\nontology: EFO:0000246\ndescription: Age of the donor at sample collection.\npattern: {Number}{Unit} where Unit is Y (years), M (months), W (weeks), D (days).\n  Also allowed: "anonymized" if age is stated but anonymized.\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: ancestry category\nsdrf_column: characteristics[ancestry category]\nontology: HANCESTRO\ndescription: Ancestry or ethnic background of the donor.\ninstructions: Use HANCESTRO terms if possible. Return null if not stated or not applicable.\n\nterm: biological replicate\nsdrf_column: characteristics[biological replicate]\nontology: MS:1001809\ndescription: Identifier for the biological replicate (integer or "pooled").\ninstructions: Return a description of the replication strategy from the paper\n  (e.g. "3 biological replicates", "2 independent experiments"). The actual\n  integer values are assigned per-file automatically; describe what you find in the text.\n\nterm: genotype\nsdrf_column: characteristics[genotype]\nontology: EFO:0000513\ndescription: Known genetic variant, mutation, or genotype of the subject.\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Example: "wild type", "Δatg5", "ΔYme1".\n\nterm: pooled sample\nsdrf_column: characteristics[pooled sample]\nontology: NCIT:C165587\ndescription: Whether the sample is a pooled sample.\nallowed_values: not pooled, pooled, or pattern SN=sample1;SN=sample2\ninstructions: Return "not pooled" unless explicitly stated otherwise.\n\nterm: phosphate\nsdrf_column: characteristics[phosphate]\nontology: PRIDE:0000805\ndescription: Phosphate concentration (only relevant for environmental/water samples).\npattern: number + mg/L or umol/L\ninstructions: Return null unless this is a water/environmental sample with explicit\n  phosphate concentration stated.\n\nterm: temperature\nsdrf_column: characteristics[temperature]\nontology: PRIDE:0000703\ndescription: Temperature at the sampling location (environmental samples only).\npattern: number + °C\ninstructions: Return null unless this is an environmental/metaproteomics sample\n  with explicit temperature stated. Do NOT confuse with crosslinking temperature.\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nAutoinhibition of the GEF activity of cytoskeletal regulatory protein Trio is disrupted in neurodevelopmental disorder-related genetic variants\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nTRIO encodes a cytoskeletal regulatory protein with three catalytic domains—two guanine exchange factor (GEF) domains, GEF1 and GEF2, and a kinase domain—as well as several accessory domains that have not been extensively studied. Function-damaging variants in the TRIO gene are known to be enriched in individuals with neurodevelopmental disorders (NDDs). Disease variants in the GEF1 domain or the nine adjacent spectrin repeats (SRs) are enriched in NDDs, suggesting that dysregulated GEF1 activity is linked to these disorders. We provide evidence here that the Trio SRs interact intramolecularly with the GEF1 domain to inhibit its enzymatic activity. We demonstrate that SRs 6-9 decrease GEF1 catalytic activity both in\xa0vitro and in cells and show that NDD-associated variants in the SR8 and GEF1 domains relieve this autoinhibitory constraint. Our results from chemical cross-linking and bio-layer interferometry indicate that the SRs primarily contact the pleckstrin homology region of the GEF1 domain, reducing GEF1 binding to the small GTPase Rac1. Together, our findings reveal a key regulatory mechanism that is commonly disrupted in multiple NDDs and may offer a new target for therapeutic intervention for TRIO-associated NDDs.\n\n[METHODS]\nExperimental procedures\nExpression construct cloning and protein purification\nHuman Trio SR6-GEF1 was PCR amplified and inserted into the pFastBac1 HTa vector (Invitrogen). Site-directed mutagenesis was used to insert point mutations into pFastBac1-Hta-SR6-GEF1 construct and confirmed by DNA sequencing. Primers used for cloning are included in Table\xa0S1.\nRecombinant baculoviruses were generated using Sf9 cells (Bac-to-Bac expression system, Thermo Fisher Scientific). Baculoviruses were used to infect Hi5 cells at an estimated multiplicity of infection\xa0= 1 for 48\xa0h before lysis in lysis buffer (20\xa0mM Hepes pH 7.25, 500\xa0mM KCl, 5\xa0mM β-mercaptoethanol, 5% glycerol, 1% TritonX-100, 20\xa0mM imidazole, 1\xa0mM DTT, 1\xa0mM PMSF, 1× Roche cOmplete protease inhibitors EDTA free) for 20\xa0min at 4 °C. Lysates were affinity purified using Ni-NTA resin (Qiagen) and eluted with 250\xa0mM imidazole. Elution fractions were further purified over an Sephadex 200 (S200) Increase 10/300\xa0GL column into assay buffer (20\xa0mM Hepes pH 7.25, 150\xa0mM KCl, 5% glycerol, 0.01% TritonX-100, 1\xa0mM DTT), aliquoted, and flash frozen for long-term storage.\nHuman Trio GEF1 and Rac1 were generated and affinity purified from bacterial cells as described in Blaise et\xa0al.. Point mutants were generated using site-directed mutagenesis. Following affinity purification, eluted protein was further purified over an S200 Increase column into assay buffer, aliquoted, and flash frozen for long-term storage.\nStokes radii of proteins were estimated based on the elution volume from the S200 Increase column, calculated based on a standard curve generated by running protein standards (Protein Standard Mix 15–600\xa0kDa, Supelco).\nBODIPY-FL-GDP nucleotide exchange assays\n12.8\xa0μM Rac1 was loaded with 3.2\xa0μM BODIPY-FL-GDP (Invitrogen) in 1× assay buffer (20\xa0mM Hepes pH 7.25, 150\xa0mM KCl, 5% glycerol, 1\xa0mM DTT, 0.01% TritonX-100) plus 2\xa0mM EDTA to a total volume of 25\xa0μl per reaction, then incubated for 1\xa0h at room temperature. BODIPY-FL-GDP loading onto Rac1 was halted by the addition of 5\xa0μl of MgCl2, for a total reaction volume of 30\xa0μl with a final MgCl2 concentration of 5\xa0mM. Prior to initiating the reaction with 100\xa0nM Trio GEF, 30\xa0μl of GTPase (12.8\xa0μM) plus MgCl2 (5\xa0mM) mix or blank (3.2\xa0μM BODIPY-FL-GDP, 2\xa0mM EDTA, and 1× assay buffer) was added to appropriate wells. During the BODIPY-FL-GDP loading incubation period, GEF1-containing proteins were prepared in 1× assay buffer, 4\xa0mM GTP, and 2\xa0mM MgCl2. Exchange reactions were initiated by adding 10\xa0μl of 100\xa0nM Trio GEF mixture (as stated above) to each well, for a total reaction volume of 40\xa0μl. Real-time fluorescence data was measured every 10\xa0s for 30\xa0min monitoring BODIPY-FL fluorescence by excitation at 488\xa0nm and emission at 535\xa0nm, as per Blaise et\xa0al..\nAll kobs measurements of GEF1 activity represent at least three experimental replicates with three technical replicates per experiment. Results are shown as the mean\xa0± SD from multiple experiments. A one-way ANOVA was used to determine statistical significance between SR6-GEF1 and all other variants (two-tailed p-value\xa0< 0.05) and adjusted using Dunnett’s multiple comparisons test. Catalytic efficiencies (kcat/KM) of selected SR6-GEF1 constructs were extracted from a linear fit of catalytic rate (kobs, s−1) versus GEF1 concentration (nM). Three experimental replicates were performed for each SR6-GEF1 construct, and the catalytic efficiency values were averaged. Results are shown as the mean\xa0± SD. A one-way ANOVA was used to determine statistical significance between SR6-GEF1 and all other variants (two-tailed p-value\xa0< 0.05) and adjusted using Dunnett’s multiple comparisons test.\nProtein structure predictions\nAlphaFold was used to access the predicted structure of human Trio spectrin repeats 1-GEF1 (amino acids 201–1600), entry number AF-O75962-F2. Swiss pdb Viewer was used to model SR6-GEF1, amino acids 788 to 1599. DISOPRED was used to predict the probability of disorder of Trio SR6-GEF1, amino acids 788 to 1599.\nLimited proteolysis\nSR6-GEF1 in assay buffer plus 10\xa0mM CaCl2 was diluted to 0.4\xa0mg/ml and incubated with increasing concentrations of trypsin (0.001\xa0mg/ml–0.11\xa0mg/ml) for 1\xa0h at room temperature in a 25\xa0μl total reaction volume. Reactions were quenched with 8\xa0μl quench buffer (50\xa0mM Tris–HCl pH 6.8, 4% SDS, 10% glycerol, 0.1% bromophenol blue, 5% β-mercaptoethanol, 1\xa0mM PMSF, 4\xa0mM EGTA, 4\xa0mM EDTA) and immediately boiled for 10\xa0min. Samples were immediately run on a 12% SDS-PAGE gel, and proteins were visualized by Coomassie R250 staining.\nMajor gel bands were excised and washed with 50:50 acetonitrile:water buffer containing 100\xa0mM ammonium bicarbonate. Proteins in the gel were reduced with 4.5\xa0mM DTT at 37 °C for 20\xa0min and alkylated with 10\xa0mM iodoacetamide at room temperature for 20\xa0min in the dark. Gel bands were washed twice with 50:50 acetonitrile:water containing 100\xa0mM bicarbonate and dried for 10\xa0min in a SpeedVac. Trypsin digestion was carried out (1:100\xa0M ratio of trypsin to protein) by incubation with the gel piece at 37 °C overnight. The digest samples were analyzed by LC–MS/MS using a Q-Exactive Plus mass spectrometer equipped with a Waters nanoACQUITY ultra-performance liquid chromatography system using a Waters Symmetry C18 180\xa0μm by 20\xa0mm trap column and a 1.7\xa0μm (75\xa0μm inner diameter by 250\xa0mm) nanoACQUITY ultra-performance liquid chromatography column (35 °C) for peptide separation. Trapping was done at 15\xa0μl/min with 99% buffer A (100% water, 0.1% formic acid) for 1\xa0min. Peptide separation was performed at 300\xa0nl/min with buffer A and buffer B (100% acetonitrile, 0.1% formic acid) over a linear gradient. High-Energy collisional dissociation was utilized to fragment peptide ions via data-dependent acquisition. Mass spectral data were processed with Proteome Discoverer (v. 2.3) and protein database search was carried out in Mascot search engine (Matrix Science, LLC; v. 2.6.0). Protein searches were conducted against the Trichoplusia ni protein database and the human Trio SR6-GEF1 sequence. Mascot search parameters included the following: parent peptide ion tolerance of 10.0\xa0ppm; peptide fragment ion mass tolerance of 0.020\xa0Da; strict trypsin fragments (enzyme cleavage after the C terminus of K or R, but not if it is followed by P); fixed modification of carbamidomethyl (C); and variable modification of phospho (S,\xa0T, Y), oxidation (M), and propioamidation (C), and deamidation (NQ). Peptide identification confidence was set at 95% confidence probability based on Mascot MOWSE score. Results were transferred to Scaffold software (Proteome Software; v. 4) for further data analysis to look at peptide abundances in reference to their start position. These were utilized to plot in a frequency distribution to determine band identity.\nCross-linking mass spectrometry\nCross-linking experiments were performed as in Sanchez et\xa0al. with deviations noted below. Twenty five micrograms of protein was incubated in assay buffer with 100\xa0μM BS3 (Thermo Fisher) for 30\xa0min on ice. The reaction was quenched by adding Tris pH 7.25 to 10\xa0mM final concentration. Protein was then acetone precipitated and the pellet was alkylated with iodoacetamide and digested with trypsin. Peptides were desalted on a 100\xa0μl Omix C18 tip (Agilent), dried, and reconstituted in 100\xa0μl of 0.1% formic acid. Mass spectrometry was performed on an Orbitrap Exploris 480 equipped with an EasySpray nanoESI source, an EasySpray 75\xa0μm\xa0× 15\xa0cm C18 column, and a FAIMS Pro ion mobility interface coupled with an UltiMate 3000 RSLCnano system (Thermo Scientific). Each sample was analyzed at four different FAIMS compensation voltages (CV\xa0=\xa0−40 V,\xa0−50 V,\xa0−60 V,\xa0−70 V) to provide gas-phase enrichment/fractionation of cross-linked peptide ions. Each analysis was a separate injection (2.5\xa0μl sample). The sample was loaded at 2% B at 600\xa0nl/min for 35\xa0min followed by a multisegment elution gradient to 35% B at 200\xa0nl/min over 70\xa0min with the remaining time used for column washing and reequilibration (buffer A: 0.1% formic acid (aq); buffer B: 0.1% formic acid in acetonitrile). Precursor ions were acquired at 120,000 resolving power, and ions with charges 3 to 8+ were isolated in the quadrupole using a 1.6\xa0m/z unit window and dissociated by HCD at 30% NCE. Product ions were measured at 30,000 resolving power. Peak lists were generated using PAVA (in house Python app), searched with Protein Prospector v6.3.23, and classified as unique residue pairs using Touchstone (an in-house R library) at SVM.score ≥1.5 corresponding to a residue pair level FDR\xa0<\xa00.1% and then further summarized and presented as domain-domain pairs using Touchstone. A custom database consisting of the human Trio construct and a 10× longer decoy database (11 sequences total) was used in the Prospector search, using tryptic specificity with 2-missed cleavages and tolerance of 10/25\xa0ppm (precursor/product). DSS/BS3 cross-linking was specified.\nBio-layer interferometry\nKinetic binding assays were performed using a ForteBio BLItz instrument. Ni-NTA biosensors were prehydrated in assay buffer for 10\xa0min prior to the experiment. Biosensors were first measured for a baseline signal for 30\xa0s before loading His-GEF1 (0.5\xa0μM) or SR6-GEF1 (2\xa0μM) in assay buffer for 5\xa0min (concentrations were optimized for reproducible biosensor loading and signal change). Biosensors were then re-equilibrated in assay buffer for 30\xa0s before introducing varying concentrations of Rac1 (at least four concentrations per experiment) in assay buffer for 5\xa0min to measure association. Association curves were fit to a one phase exponential curve to obtain a kobs value and these values were plotted against Rac1 concentration to calculate a Kd from the linear fit of this line, where the y-intercept\xa0= koff and slope\xa0= kon (Kd\xa0= koff/kon). Concentration gradients were replicated at least three times independently, and the Kd measurements of each interaction were compared using an unpaired t test. Reported values are mean\xa0± SD.\nMeasurement of GEF and SR6-GEF1 impact on cell morphology\nPEI was used to transfect HEK293\xa0cells with 0.5 to 4\xa0μg of DNA in 6-well dishes at a density of 3\xa0× 105\xa0cells per well. Twenty four hours after transfection, cells were trypsinized and replated at a density of 2.5\xa0× 104\xa0cells per coverslip on fibronectin-coated coverslips (10\xa0μg/ml fibronectin). Twenty four hours post plating, cells were fixed and stained as in Lim et\xa0al.. Cells were fixed for 5\xa0min in 2% paraformaldehyde in cytoskeleton buffer (10\xa0mM MES pH 6.8, 138\xa0mM KCl, 3\xa0mM MgCl2, 2\xa0mM EGTA, 320\xa0mM sucrose). Cells were rinsed three times in Tris Buffered Saline (TBS) (20\xa0mM Tris pH 7.4, 150\xa0mM NaCl) and incubated with 5\xa0μg/ml Alexa Fluor Wheat Germ Agglutinin 555 in TBS (Thermo Fisher) for 10\xa0min to visualize the cell membrane when imaging. Cells were washed another three times in TBS, then permeabilized for 10\xa0min in 0.3% TritonX-100/TBS and washed another three times in 0.1% TritonX-100/TBS. Cells were blocked for 30\xa0min in antibody dilution buffer (ADB) (0.1% TritonX-100, 2% bovine serum albumin, 0.1% NaN3, 10% fetal bovine serum, TBS) and incubated with primary antibody (ADB containing a 1:2000 dilution of Goat Anti-GFP, Rockland) at 4 °C overnight. The next morning, cells were washed in 0.1% TritonX-100/TBS three times and incubated in secondary antibody for 1\xa0h at room temperature (in ADB, 1:2000 Alexa Fluor 488 Donkey Anti-Goat, Abcam). Cells were washed once in 0.1% TritonX-100/TBS, once in TBS, and then mounted onto glass slides using AquaMount (Lerner Laboratories). After drying, coverslips were sealed using clear nail polish and imaged using a 40× objective on a spinning disk confocal microscope (UltraVIEW VoX spinning disk confocal (PerkinElmer) Nikon Ti-E-Eclipse), collecting a full z-stack of images for each cell. Identical microscope settings were used between imaging samples.\nAfter imaging cells, images were processed using Fiji/ImageJ to generate a sum projection of the GFP channel for quantifying fluorescence as a proxy for total protein expression. Images were then analyzed using CellProfiler to semi-automatically detect cell edges and compute cell area. Cell area was normalized for protein expression on a single cell basis by dividing the total area of the cell by the total GFP fluorescence of the cell (a proxy for total protein expression). Two biological replicates were performed, with 25 to 40\xa0cells quantified per group per replicate. Statistical significance of differences in the normalized cell area was determined using a one-way ANOVA between the GFP control and all other groups (two-tailed p-value\xa0< 0.05) and adjusted using Dunnett’s multiple comparisons test.\n\n[RESULTS]\nResults\nInclusion of SRs 6-9 reduces Trio GEF1 activity\nGenetic variants in SRs 6-9 are associated with NDDs, some of which were previously shown to affect Trio-mediated Rac1 activation in cells. To measure the impact of the SRs on GEF1 activity in\xa0vitro, we generated and purified Trio GEF1 alone (42\xa0kDa) and a Trio fragment containing SRs 6-9 appended to the GEF1 domain (SR6-GEF1, 99\xa0kDa) (Fig.\xa01A). Both proteins were monodisperse upon size-exclusion chromatography and eluted at a position consistent with being monomers (estimated Stokes radius was 3.8\xa0nm for GEF1, 5.6\xa0nm for SR6-GEF1) (Fig.\xa01B). Using a fluorescence-based guanine nucleotide exchange assay, we measured the catalytic activity of GEF1 and SR6-GEF1. Purified 100\xa0nM GEF1 efficiently catalyzed exchange of BODIPY-FL-GDP for GTP on Rac1, with a first-order dissociation rate constant kobs\xa0= 2.4\xa0± 0.6\xa0× 10−3 s−1 (Fig.\xa01, C and D). Measurement of the rate constant, kobs, as a function of GEF1 concentration yielded a kcat/KM\xa0= 1.9\xa0× 104\xa0M−1\xa0s−1 (Fig.\xa01, E and F). SR6-GEF1 similarly promoted GTP exchange onto Rac1 but with a significantly reduced (∼20 fold and 6-fold, respectively) kobs\xa0= 1.2\xa0± 1.8\xa0×10−4 s−1 and kcat/KM\xa0= 3.1\xa0× 103\xa0M−1\xa0s−1 (Fig.\xa01, C, D and F). These data indicate that inclusion of SRs 6-9 inhibits Trio GEF1 activity for Rac1 in\xa0vitro.\nNDD-associated variants in SR8 increase Trio GEF1 activity in the context of SR6-GEF1\nWe generated and purified SR6-GEF1 expression constructs containing single NDD-associated variants in SR8 and measured their ability to catalyze nucleotide exchange on Rac1 (Fig.\xa02, A and B). When tested at 100\xa0nM, all SR8 variants, except N1080I, increased the kobs by 4 to 8 fold over that of WT SR6-GEF1 (Fig.\xa02, C and D). In agreement with these findings, one representative SR8 variant, SR6-GEF1R1078Q, which had a significantly increased kobs\xa0= 1.0\xa0± 0.5\xa0× 10−3 s−1, had a kcat/KM\xa0= 4.7\xa0× 103\xa0M−1\xa0s−1, a 1.5-fold increase in catalytic efficiency over WT SR6-GEF1 (Fig.\xa02E). These findings indicate that NDD-associated variants in SR8 are sufficient to relieve SR autoinhibition.\nNDD-associated variants in SR6 decrease GEF1 activity in the context of SR6-GEF1\nWe also generated two SR6-GEF1 constructs harboring individual disease variants in the SR6 domain. While the rate constant (kobs) values obtained for each construct did not significantly decrease compared to WT SR6-GEF1, measurement of catalytic efficiency, kcat/KM, of both WT SR6-GEF1 and SR6-GEF1E883D revealed that SR6-GEF1E883D had a significantly decreased catalytic efficiency of a kcat/KM\xa0= 1.7\xa0× 103\xa0M−1\xa0s−1, 1.8-fold lower than WT SR6-GEF1 (Fig.\xa02E). This suggests that NDD-associated variants in SR6 decrease GEF1 activity.\nGEF1 variant D1368V increases GEF activity only in the context of SR6-GEF1\nHypothesizing that the SRs might contact GEF1 to impact catalytic activity, we searched for GEF1 domain variants that might impact potential autoinhibition of GEF1 activity by SRs. Unlike GEF1 disease variants that lie in the GEF1:Rac1 interface and decrease GEF1 activity, D1368V lies in the DH domain but is distal to the GEF1:Rac1 interface, so its impact is less well understood (Fig.\xa03A). However, introduction of the D1368V variant greatly potentiates the ability of the Trio9 splice isoform, which contains all of the SRs, to increase activity of a Rac1 reporter in cells. We introduced D1368V into SR6-GEF1 and found that it significantly increased catalytic activity, with a kobs\xa0= 1.4\xa0± 0.3\xa0× 10−3 s−1 and kcat/KM\xa0= 4.8\xa0× 103\xa0M−1\xa0s−1 (Fig.\xa03, B–E), a 1.5-fold increase over the kcat/KM for WT SR6-GEF1. In contrast, introducing D1368V into GEF1 alone did not impact its activity compared to GEF1 (Fig.\xa03, B–E), indicating that the activating effects of D1368V require SRs 6-9. Together with data reported above, these are consistent with a model in which NDD-associated variants in SR8 and GEF1 relieve inhibition of GEF1 activity by the SRs.\nThe SRs and GEF1 form distinct stable interacting domains\nWe used AlphaFold to model human Trio SR6-GEF1 (Fig.\xa04, A and B). Strikingly, this model suggests that SRs interact with the GEF1 domain, with SR8 closely apposed to GEF1 and the NDD-associated mutations concentrated at this SR8:GEF1 interface. This model of SR6-GEF1 and additional analysis using DISOPRED predicted the existence of an unstructured loop between SR9 and GEF1, suggesting this flexible region may connect the SRs and GEF1 domain (Fig.\xa04C). We used limited proteolysis to probe for the presence of a flexible linker between SR9 and the GEF1 domain that might be susceptible to partial proteolysis. Treatment of SR6-GEF1 at intermediate levels of trypsin yielded two major bands, identified by mass spectrometry as composed of SRs 6-9 and GEF1, respectively. This observation indicates that SRs 6-9 and the GEF1 domain each make up distinct folding units with increased relative resistance to protease (Fig.\xa04D). Together, these findings support a model in which the SRs make contact with GEF1.\nTo test directly for possible interactions between the SRs and GEF1 domain, we incubated SR6-GEF1 with an 11.4\xa0Å spacer lysine cross-linker, BS3 (bis(sulfosuccinimidyl)suberate), and analyzed cross-linked peptides via mass spectrometry to identify sites in close enough proximity to cross-link. Several long-distance cross-links were observed between the SRs and the GEF1 domain (Fig.\xa05A). Specifically, the SR:GEF1 interface includes a peptide in DH domain which is directly at the Rac1 binding interface (1429–1438, green in Fig.\xa05A) and a peptide in the PH domain important for stabilizing the Rac1 interaction (1529–1537, orange in Fig.\xa05A) (Fig.\xa05A). Multiple regions originating in SR6-9 contact these peptides in the GEF domain. This suggests that SR6-GEF1 may be dynamic, with multiple conformational states captured by cross-linking. We hypothesize that these SR:GEF1 contacts likely disrupt Rac1 binding to GEF1\nWe also performed chemical cross-linking on three variants in SR6-GEF1 to understand how intramolecular contacts may change in the variants. The SR6-GEF1 variants that display activated GEF activity, R1078Q and D1368V, both exhibited a loss of contact between SR6, 7, 9, and the GEF1 domain (Fig.\xa05B). In addition, R1078Q, but not D1368V, also reduced SR8:GEF1 contacts (Table\xa0S2). In contrast, the SR6 variant, E883D, which reduced GEF activity, did not reduce intramolecular contacts with GEF1; in fact, new contacts appeared (SR7 and SR9 contacts, blue and purple arrowheads, Fig.\xa05B), suggesting this variant may reinforce intramolecular SR:GEF contacts (Fig.\xa05B). These data are consistent with a model in which specific intramolecular contacts between the SRs and GEF1 are altered in genetic variants with increased GEF1 activity.\nThe SRs reduce GEF1 binding to Rac1\nBased on our cross-linking data, we hypothesized that an interaction between SRs 6-9 and PH1 may impair the ability of GEF1 to bind Rac1. We used bio-layer interferometry to measure the association of nucleotide-free Rac1 with His-GEF1 or His-SR6-GEF1 immobilized on a nitrilotriacetic acid (Ni-NTA) affinity chip. GEF1 bound to Rac1 with a Kd\xa0= 151\xa0± 49\xa0nM in nucleotide-free conditions (Fig.\xa06, A–C). SR6-GEF1 had a reduced affinity for Rac1, with a Kd\xa0= 316\xa0± 87\xa0nM (Fig.\xa06, A–C). Taken together with the cross-linking data, this supports a model where the SRs contact the PH domain to impair GEF1 binding to Rac1, which likely contributes to the reduction in observed GEF1 activity.\nSRs 6-9 inhibit GEF1-induced cell spreading\nTrio GEF1 activates Rac1 and RhoG to coordinate downstream cytoskeletal changes and mediate changes in cell morphology. We first expressed Trio GEF1-GFP in HEK293\xa0cells and quantified its impact on cell morphology (Fig.\xa07, A–C). When matched for GFP expression levels, GEF1 expressing cells had significantly increased cell area compared to GFP controls (Fig.\xa07, A–C). Cells expressing GEF1 appeared to be more spread with round lamellipodia encompassing the cell edge, a common result of Rac1 activation (Fig.\xa07B). The area of cells expressing a catalytic-dead mutant of GEF1, GEF1 ND/AA (N1465A/D1466A), were similar to GFP controls, indicating a key role for GEF1 catalytic activity in this morphological change. In contrast to GEF1, SR6-GEF1 expressing cells had no measurable effect on cell area, but the SR8 mutant, SR6-GEF1R1078Q, increased cell area over that of GFP and SR6-GEF1 WT (Fig.\xa07, B and C). Cells expressing SR6-GEF1R1078Q also appeared qualitatively similar in morphology to those cells expressing GEF1 alone, with more full, rounded edges (Fig.\xa07B). Therefore, inclusion of SRs 6-9 inhibits Trio GEF1-dependent changes in cell morphology, and disease-associated variants can disrupt this inhibitory regulation.\nWe then expressed GFP-Trio9s, a predominant neuronal isoform throughout neurodevelopment, in HEK293\xa0cells and quantified its impact on cell morphology (Fig.\xa07, A, D and E). Interestingly, when matched for GFP expression levels, GFP-Trio9s expressing cells had significantly decreased cell area compared to GFP controls. Expressing two variants of Trio9s, the most activated SR8 mutant, GFP-Trio9sR1078Q, and a catalytic-dead mutant of GEF1, GFP-Trio9s ND/AA (N1465A/D1466A), decreased cell area compared to GFP alone (Fig.\xa07, D and E). Cells expressing any variant of GFP-Trio9s appeared very round, completely lacking lamellipodia or cell edge protrusions (Fig.\xa07, D and E). We speculate that activity of the Trio GEF2 domain, which targets RhoA to promote cytoskeleton contractility, may dominate in this context, making it difficult to discern specific effects on GEF1 activity.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
